<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bunce Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bunce Island is a small island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> River, upstream from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>. From the 1670s it held a fortified British slave-trading post, run in the 18th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> merchants such as Grant, Oswald and Company, which shipped tens of thousands of captive Africans across the Atlantic, many to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Its ruins are now a protected historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bunce-island.jpg" alt="Bunce Island" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cnes - Spot Image · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freetown_SPOT_1094.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
